<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Indra is the king of the gods in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, the god of thunder and rain who wields the thunderbolt and slays the serpent Vritra to release the waters. He is the most invoked deity of the Vedic hymns. His name, with those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitra">Mitra</a>, Varuna and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashvins">Nasatyas</a>, appears in a fourteenth-century BCE treaty between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, evidence of Indo-Aryan speakers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indra.jpg" alt="Indra" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indra,_Chief_of_the_Gods_LACMA_M.69.13.4_(1_of_5).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Royal religion was Hindu, the brick sanctuaries of Isanapura centred on lingas, with Harihara, Brahma, <b>Indra</b> and Lakshmi also worshipped and Buddhism tolerated beside them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chenla">Chenla · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>A Mitanni treaty with the Hittites calls on the gods Mitra, Varuna, <b>Indra</b> and the Nasatyas, some of the earliest evidence for Indo-Aryan languages anywhere.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mitanni-empire">Mitanni Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>A collateral branch descended from Govinda III’s brother <b>Indra</b> governed southern Gujarat from c. 800.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The hymns of the Rigveda, composed in an early form of Sanskrit perhaps between 1500 and 1200 BCE, praise gods of nature and order such as <b>Indra</b>, Agni and Varuna and accompany sacrifices performed by priests around a sacred fire.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Indo-Aryan words also appear far to the west: a treaty of about 1380 BCE between the Hittites and the kingdom of Mitanni in Syria invokes the gods Mitra, Varuna and <b>Indra</b>, and a Mitanni manual on horse training uses Indo-Aryan numbers.</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Ventan, identified with <b>Indra</b>, Korravai, identified with Durga, and Varunan, the sea god, also appear.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sangam-age-tamilakam">Sangam-age Tamilakam · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
